 \begin{abstract}
-Large language models (LLMs) can describe how travelers might react to delays, fares or rain, but querying them for every agent in a city-scale simulation remains costly. Distilling their judgments into compact traveler models removes this cost and raises a question that predictive accuracy does not answer, namely whether the distilled model changes its choices in the same way as its source when conditions change. This study evaluates LLM-derived traveler models by their responses to paired changes in travel conditions and follows these responses through comparisons with the LLM Teacher, with stated choices and with simulated network demand. On a held-out benchmark of six synthetic personas and three training seeds, paired response supervision lowers probability-response error by 4.97\% relative to pointwise distribution matching, and a multinomial logit Student lowers it by a further 14.30\%. Combining that choice model with an independent neural departure predictor gives a departure error of 7.61 minutes against the Teacher, within the range of jointly trained neural models. Stated-choice data from 332 Singapore and 321 Shanghai respondents show that agreement with people depends on the intervention, and the model with the smallest Teacher error reverses the stated response to poorer public-transport accessibility. Contemporary Teacher predictions for 24 respondents per city distinguish Teacher--human from Student--Teacher discrepancies on the same tasks. In a 1,000-traveler Helsinki simulation, feasibility-constrained assignment makes more trips routable without narrowing the gap between predicted and simulated responses. Fidelity at one level therefore need not carry over to the next, and the choice of traveler model should follow the intervention and behavioral outcome that a simulation is meant to inform.
+Transport scenario analysis requires traveler models that reproduce changes in choices, not only baseline predictions. For models distilled from large language models (LLMs), agreement with a Teacher does not establish agreement with travelers or preservation of responses in simulation. We develop a response-centric evaluation that links three comparisons: paired Teacher--Student contrasts, agreement with stated choices, and transmission through assignment and network execution. A persona-disjoint benchmark from one LLM Teacher contains 398 test response pairs across six synthetic personas, evaluated with three neural training seeds. Explicit response supervision reduces mean absolute probability-response error from 0.06930 to 0.06586; a multinomial logit Student reduces it further to 0.05644. Pairing the logit model with independent neural timing yields a departure error of 7.61 minutes against the Teacher. The model ranking changes when evaluated on stated-choice convenience samples of 332 Singapore and 321 Shanghai respondents. For poorer public-transport accessibility in Singapore, the Teacher-best Student predicts a 13.85-percentage-point increase in PT use, whereas respondents report a 24.40-point decrease. A 1,000-traveler Helsinki execution experiment further separates response preservation from routability: feasibility-constrained assignment removes unroutable PT assignments without reducing the response gap under a perceived-delay perturbation. These results establish distinct tests for compression fidelity, agreement with stated behavior and execution consistency. Model selection for transport simulation should evaluate the relevant intervention response at each level rather than rely on a single imitation or prediction score.
 \end{abstract}
 
