 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
-Transport scenario analysis is concerned less with where travelers go today than with how their choices would change if conditions changed. A public-transport delay may push some commuters into a car, leave others on the same service and lead others to depart earlier. Rain, higher fares and longer walks to a stop produce different reactions, even among people making similar trips. Agent-based simulation represents how such decisions interact with road and transit supply \citep{horni2016multi,axhausen2002some}, and the policy value of a simulated scenario therefore rests on the change in demand that its behavioral model generates, not only on the baseline mode shares it reproduces.
+Transport scenario analysis asks how travel demand changes when conditions change. Matching a baseline mode share does not establish whether a model will reproduce the response to a delay, a fare increase or a longer walk to a stop. In agent-based simulation, such responses pass through assignment, routing and interaction with transport supply before becoming simulated demand \citep{horni2016multi,axhausen2002some}. The relevant behavioral target is therefore a change between conditions, alongside the level of demand in each condition.
 
-Large language models (LLMs) have become an attractive source for this behavioral component. A single prompt can combine a traveler's circumstances, the available modes and the attributes of a trip with a change in context, and recent studies have used this flexibility for travel-behavior prediction, route-choice agents and agent-based transport modeling \citep{mo2026llmtravel,wang2025agentic,liu2025framework,nie2025roles}. Two obstacles stand between these demonstrations and city-scale use. Querying an LLM for every agent and scenario is slow and costly, and it remains unclear how closely LLM judgments track the behavior of actual travelers. \citet{liu2025framework} identify both issues as open problems for LLM-agent modeling and suggest hybrid designs that combine LLM agents with conventional models as a near-term route. Behavioral distillation is one such design. The LLM acts as a Teacher that supplies judgments offline, and a compact numerical Student makes the repeated decisions that a simulation requires.
+Large language models (LLMs) offer a flexible way to specify traveler behavior. Prompts can combine personal circumstances, trip attributes and contextual changes, supporting applications in travel prediction, route choice and agent-based modeling \citep{mo2026llmtravel,wang2025agentic,liu2025framework,nie2025roles}. Behavioral distillation moves repeated inference offline: an LLM Teacher supplies numerical judgments, and a compact Student uses these targets to make local predictions. This is a concrete instance of the hybrid LLM--conventional-model designs proposed for transport simulation \citep{liu2025framework}. It reduces reliance on live LLM calls during deployment, but introduces a distinct validation problem: which properties of the Teacher survive compression, and which of those properties are useful for transport analysis?
 
-Distillation shifts attention to a question that predictive accuracy does not settle, namely which properties of the Teacher the Student must retain. Evidence from outside distillation suggests that the answer is not obvious. LLMs used as synthetic survey respondents can reproduce aggregate patterns of human answers \citep{argyle2023out,aher2023using}, yet their answers may show too little variation and depend on prompt wording and model version \citep{bisbee2024synthetic}. In travel choice experiments, LLMs recover the direction of value-of-time effects more reliably than their sensitivity to context and income \citep{valuingtime2026}. Machine-learning choice models raise a parallel concern, because high predictive accuracy does not guarantee plausible elasticities, substitution patterns or other behavioral quantities \citep{wang2020deep,martinbaos2023prediction}. In each case the property most relevant to policy, sensitivity to a change in conditions, is also the property that accuracy measures least directly.
+The distinction matters because predictive agreement is not behavioral agreement. In travel choice modeling, models with strong predictive performance can differ in elasticities and other behavioral indicators \citep{wang2020deep,martinbaos2023prediction}. LLM-based travel studies likewise distinguish plausible responses from empirically aligned behavior \citep{valuingtime2026,liu2026persona,xu2026satisfaction}. Distillation introduces another layer: even a Student that resembles its Teacher at individual states may differ in how its predictions change between states. Conversely, a less faithful Student may accidentally agree more closely with human responses. Neither model compression nor improved prediction alone resolves this issue.
 
-Two targets of distillation can therefore be distinguished. \emph{Static fidelity} refers to agreement between Student and Teacher at individual traveler states. \emph{Response fidelity} refers to agreement in the change of mode-choice probabilities between paired states that hold the traveler and trip fixed (Figure~\ref{fig:concept}). Two models can share the same most likely mode at baseline and still redistribute probability very differently when a delay or fare increase is introduced. For scenario analysis, the second kind of agreement determines how a modeled intervention changes demand.
+We distinguish \emph{static fidelity}, agreement with the Teacher at individual states, from \emph{response fidelity}, agreement in finite changes of elicited choice distributions for the same traveler and trip. These contrasts are defined for specified perturbations; they are not, by themselves, population causal effects or elasticities. Figure~\ref{fig:concept} illustrates the traveler--trip pairing and its connection to aggregate demand. Exact agreement at every endpoint would imply exact response agreement. In finite data and finite-capacity models, however, average endpoint scores do not directly evaluate the contrasts that a scenario analysis queries.
 
 \begin{figure}[pos=htbp]
 \centering\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth,keepaspectratio]{figures/narrative/figure1_response_concept.pdf}
-\caption{Traveler profiles, contextual changes and aggregate demand responses. Each scenario pair holds the traveler and trip fixed. The profiles are illustrative. The bars show mode assignments before routing for 10,000 synthetic Singapore travelers under the supply-adapted Student introduced in Section~\ref{sec:architectures}, rounded to whole percentages. Poorer accessibility is evaluated separately. Scenario definitions and exact shares are given in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-tab:singapore}.}
+\caption{Traveler--trip pairing and illustrative demand responses. Each pair changes travel conditions while holding the traveler and trip fixed. Profiles are illustrative; bars are rounded, pre-routing mode assignments for 10,000 synthetic Singapore travelers, not survey observations. They use the supply-adapted model defined in Section~\ref{sec:architectures}. Poorer accessibility is evaluated separately. Scenario definitions and exact shares are in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-tab:singapore}.}
